    "file": "page-20.txt",
    "text": "I\n\nThere is very good moonlight tonight.\n\nI have not seen it for more than thirty years. Seeing it\ntoday, I feel extraordinarily refreshed. Only now do I realize\nthat throughout those thirty years I was in a daze. Yet I must\nbe very careful. Otherwise, why did the Zhao family's dog look\nat me twice?\n\nI have reason to be afraid.\n\nII\n\nThere is no moonlight at all today. I know something is wrong.\nThis morning I went out cautiously, and old Mr. Zhao's eyes\nlooked strange, as though he feared me, or wanted to harm me.\nSeven or eight others were whispering about me too. They\nopened their mouths and smiled at me. A chill ran from my head\nto my heels, and I knew that all their arrangements were\nready.\n\nBut I was not afraid. I went on my way. A group of children\nahead were discussing me too. Their eyes were just like old\nMr. Zhao's, and their faces were livid. I wondered what\nquarrel I could have with children, that they should behave\nthis way. I could not help crying out, \"Tell me!\" But they ran\naway.\n\nI thought: what quarrel have I with old Mr. Zhao, or with\nthose people in the road? Twenty years ago, that was all, I\nkicked Mr. Gu Jiu's old account book, and Mr. Gu Jiu was very\ndispleased. Although old Mr. Zhao does not know him, he must\nhave heard about it and taken up the grievance on his behalf,\nagreeing with the people in the road to become my enemies. But\nthe children? They had not even been born then. Why do they\nstare at me with those strange eyes today, as though they fear\nme, as though they want to harm me? This really frightens me,\nand leaves me bewildered and sad.\n\nI understand. Their parents taught them!\n\nIII\n\nI cannot sleep at night. Everything must be studied before it\ncan be understood.\n\nSome of them have worn the cangue at the magistrate's orders.\nSome have been slapped by the local gentry. Some have had\ntheir wives taken by the bailiffs; some have seen their\nparents driven to death by creditors. Yet their faces then\nwere never so frightened, or so fierce, as they were\nyesterday.\n\nStrangest of all was the woman in the street yesterday,\nbeating her son and saying, \"You little wretch! I must bite\nyou a few times before my anger will pass!\" But her eyes were\nlooking at me. I was startled and could not conceal it. That\nwhole group with their livid faces and bared teeth burst out\nlaughing. Chen the Fifth hurried forward and dragged me home.\n\nWhen they dragged me home, everyone in the house pretended not\nto know me. Their faces were just like the faces outside. Once\nI was in the study, they bolted the door behind me, as though\nshutting up a chicken or a duck. That made it still harder to\nunderstand what lay behind everything.\n\nA few days ago a tenant from Wolf Cub Village came to report\nthe crop failure. He told my elder brother that a great\nvillain in their village had been beaten to death, and several\npeople had dug out his heart and liver, fried them in oil, and\neaten them to strengthen their courage. I put in a word, and\nthe tenant and my brother both looked at me. Only today do I\nunderstand: their eyes were exactly the same as the eyes of\nthose people outside.\n\nThinking of it, I feel cold from head to heel.\n\nIf they can eat people, they may very well eat me.\n",
