    "text": "In an earlier essay entitled The Nothingness of\nPersonality, I developed, in many of its implications,\nthe same thought that these lines are intended to\nexplain. But that essay, too much afflicted with\nliterature, is only a series of suggestions and\nexamples arranged without continuity of argument. To\nremedy that defect, I have decided to set out in the\nfollowing lines the hypothesis that led me to write\nit. In this way, with the reader beside me at the very\nsource of my thinking, feeling the difficulties\ntogether as they arise, and letting meditation run\nfreely through a single channel, we shall undertake\ntogether that eternal adventure which is the\nmetaphysical problem.\n\nMy stimulus was Berkeley's idealism. For the benefit\nof those readers in whose memory that speculation does\nnot stand out clearly, either because much time has\npassed since some professor pointed it out to their\nindifference, attacking it with disbelief, or, more\nexcusably, because they have never encountered it, we\nshould briefly recapitulate the substance of that\ndoctrine.\n\nEsse rerum est percipi: perceptibility is the being of\nthings; things exist only insofar as they are\nperceived. On that brilliant truism rests and rises\nthe illustrious structure of Berkeley's system. With\nthat brief formula he dispels the deceits of dualism\nand reveals that reality is not a distant, unfriendly\nriddle, decipherable only with difficulty, but an\nintimate nearness, easy and open on every side. Let us\nexamine the details of his argument.\n\nLet us choose any concrete idea: for example, the one\ndesignated by the word fig tree. Clearly the concept\nso labelled is only an abbreviation of many different\nperceptions. For our eyes the fig tree is a stunted,\ntwisted trunk spreading upward into bright foliage;\nfor our hands it is the rounded hardness of the wood\nand the roughness of the leaves; for our palate only\nthe desirable taste of the fruit exists. There are\nalso perceptions of smell and hearing, which I\ndeliberately leave aside so as not to entangle the\nmatter too much, but which must not be forgotten.\n\nAll of these, the unphilosophical man declares, are\ndifferent qualities of the tree. But if we go deeply\ninto this simple assertion, we shall be startled by\nthe multitude of obscurities and contradictions it\nconceals.\n\nThus, while everyone admits that greenness is not an\nessential quality of the fig tree, since its\nbrightness fades at nightfall, the leaves turn yellow\nand the trunk becomes black and dark, everyone agrees\nin asserting that convexity and volume are intimate\nrealities of the tree. With taste, the matter changes\na little. Nobody maintains that the taste of a fruit\nhas no need of our palate in order to exist in its\nfull intensity. From distinction to distinction, we\napproach the dualism now supported by physics: a\ncompromise which, according to the precise definition\nof the English Hegelian Francis Bradley, consists in\ntreating some qualities as substantives of reality and\nothers as adjectives.\n\nAs a general rule, substantial existence is attributed\nonly to extension. The remaining qualities, colour,\ntaste and sound, are considered to occupy a borderland\nbetween spirit and matter: an intermediate or\nneighbouring universe which spatial reality and our\norgans of perception fashion together, in continuous\nand secret collaboration.\n\nThat conjecture suffers from the gravest defects. The\nbare, naked extension which, according to dualists and\nmaterialists, makes up the essence of the world is a\nuseless nothingness, blind, empty, without shape or\nsize, devoid of softness and hardness: an abstraction\nthat nobody can imagine. To grant it substantial\nexistence is a desperate expedient of the\nantimetaphysical prejudice, which cannot bear to deny\naltogether the essential reality of the external\nworld, and takes refuge in the compromise of throwing\nit a verbal alms. This is a hypocrisy comparable to\nthe concept of atoms, devised merely as a defence\nagainst the idea of endless divisibility.\n",
